# How the two eyes are combined into the anaglyph, and that the choice sticks.
#
# The modes themselves are a shader composite and cannot be asserted from here —
# what this pins is the part that has broken before in other settings: that the
# command reaches the view, that a name survives the round trip it makes through
# user.json, and that a typo is refused rather than silently selecting mode zero.
#
# The mode is deliberately *not* a property of the shader. It describes the
# glasses and the eyes behind them, so it outlives a switch to a mono shader and
# back — which is the last thing asserted here.
#
#   ./tests/run_scenarios.sh tests/scenarios/anaglyph_modes.scn

new_game 9
wait_idle

# Set rather than asserted. The shipped default is gray — base.json says so, and
# it is the arrangement that tolerates imperfect glasses — but the mode is a
# user choice that persists into user.json on purpose, so by its second run this
# scenario would be starting from whatever its first run left behind. A scenario
# that asserts a default it also changes passes exactly once.
anaglyph gray
expect anaglyph gray

anaglyph dubois
expect anaglyph dubois

anaglyph half-color
expect anaglyph half-color

anaglyph color
expect anaglyph color

# Spellings are forgiving, because this is typed rather than picked from a list.
anaglyph grey
expect anaglyph gray
anaglyph half_colour
expect anaglyph half-color

# A typo is refused and leaves the mode alone. Falling back to the first enum
# value would be indistinguishable from the user having asked for gray.
anaglyph duboys
expect anaglyph half-color

# With no argument it cycles, which is how the choice is actually made: the only
# way to judge these is to look at the board through the glasses. The order runs
# gray, half-colour, Dubois, colour — increasing rivalry, so the first step off
# gray is the gentlest one.
anaglyph
expect anaglyph dubois
anaglyph
expect anaglyph color
anaglyph
expect anaglyph gray
anaglyph
expect anaglyph half-color

# Four steps along the shader list is [stereo] Red Carpet, and two more wrap back
# to mono. The mode survives both, because it is not the shader's property.
cycle shaders
cycle shaders
cycle shaders
cycle shaders
expect stereo true
expect anaglyph half-color

anaglyph dubois
cycle shaders
cycle shaders
expect stereo false
expect anaglyph dubois

# The two dials for imperfect glasses. Both default to doing nothing, both are
# clamped, and both persist — so like the mode above they are set rather than
# assumed.
anaglyph_strength 0.4
expect anaglyph_strength 0.4

# Clamped, not refused: these are tuned by eye against a moving target, so an
# overshoot should land at the limit rather than throw the setting away.
anaglyph_strength 5
expect anaglyph_strength 1
anaglyph_strength -1
expect anaglyph_strength 0
anaglyph_strength 1

# One argument sets green alone, because green through the red filter is the
# leak that actually bites.
anaglyph_leak 0.08
expect anaglyph_leak_g 0.08
expect anaglyph_leak_r 0
expect anaglyph_leak_b 0

anaglyph_leak 0.03 0.09 0.01
expect anaglyph_leak_r 0.03
expect anaglyph_leak_g 0.09
expect anaglyph_leak_b 0.01

# Past the ceiling it stops cancelling a ghost and starts cutting a hole.
anaglyph_leak 0 9 0
expect anaglyph_leak_g 0.5

# Back to doing nothing, which is a choice and must be honoured as one.
anaglyph_leak 0 0 0
expect anaglyph_leak_g 0

# The green dial: the last lever for lenses that pass green through *both*
# filters, where no assignment of green to an eye is clean.
anaglyph_green 0.5
expect anaglyph_green 0.5
anaglyph_green 9
expect anaglyph_green 1
anaglyph_green -1
expect anaglyph_green 0
anaglyph_green 1

# Which glasses are in use is a property of the viewer, so it survives a shader
# switch exactly as the mode does.
glasses red-blue
expect glasses red-blue
glasses red-cyan
expect glasses red-cyan
glasses nonsense
expect glasses red-cyan
